in{aligned}x &=2t \\\\ y &=2-t, \\\\ z &=t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484270 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t \\\\ y &=2-t, \\\\ z &=t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484286 1071 "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a & b-1\\end{aligned}|=5,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & b\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484286 1071 "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a & b-1\\end{aligned}|=5$,\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & b\\end{aligned}|.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484293 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve for $z$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y & =2\\,⟹\\,z=2+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(2+y)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(4+4y+{y}^{2}) & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2({y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+1) & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484293 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve for $z$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y & =2\\,⟹\\,z=2+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(2+y)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(4+4y+{y}^{2}) & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2({y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+1) & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484366 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{12x}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484366 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{12x}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484409 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle y=3\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).\n$$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484409 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=3\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484412 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle y=2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).\n$$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484412 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484415 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle y=4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).\n$$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484415 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484422 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=0.\n$$ Inward and outward contributions cancel because $xy$ has zero average on the circle." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484422 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} \\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=0$. Inward and outward contributions cancel because $xy$ has zero average on the circle." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484434 1074 "The speed is $2\\sqrt2\\,t$ on $0\\le t\\le1$, and $f=t^4+2t^2$. Hence $$\n\\displaystyle 2\\sqrt2\\int_0^1(t^5+2t^3)dt=4\\sqrt2/3.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484434 1074 "The speed is $2\\sqrt2\\,t$ on $0\\le t\\le1$, and $f=t^4+2t^2$. Hence $2\\sqrt2\\int_0^1(t^5+2t^3)dt=4\\sqrt2/3$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484440 1085 "Source question: Exercise **2.1.10**, [printed page 91 (PDF page 103)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=103).\n\nConsider\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\quad D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\quad E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind each expression if possible; explain why if it is not possible.\n\nPart (h) continues on printed page 92 (PDF page 104)." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484440 1085 "Source question: Exercise **2.1.10**, [printed page 91 (PDF page 103)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=103).\n\nConsider\n\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\quad D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\quad E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind each expression if possible; explain why if it is not possible.\n\nPart (h) continues on printed page 92 (PDF page 104)." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484452 1074 "Parametrize $\\mathbf r(t,z)=(\\cos t,\\sin t,z)$ with $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$ and $0\\le z\\le\\cos^2t+2\\sin^2t$. The norm of $\\mathbf r_t\\times\\mathbf r_z$ is 1. Hence the curved surface area is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\cos^2t+2\\sin^2t)dt=3\\pi.\n$$ The task asks for the cylinder wall, not its top or bottom caps." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484452 1074 "Parametrize $\\mathbf r(t,z)=(\\cos t,\\sin t,z)$ with $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$ and $0\\le z\\le\\cos^2t+2\\sin^2t$. The norm of $\\mathbf r_t\\times\\mathbf r_z$ is 1. Hence the curved surface area is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\cos^2t+2\\sin^2t)dt=3\\pi$. The task asks for the cylinder wall, not its top or bottom caps." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484458 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\cos^2t-\\sin^2t\\,dt=0.\n$$ The field is the gradient of $xy$, so positive and negative tangential contributions cancel." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484458 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} \\cos^2t-\\sin^2t\\,dt=0$. The field is the gradient of $xy$, so positive and negative tangential contributions cancel." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484470 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} 1-\\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=2\\pi.\n$$ The radial part contributes outward flow; the extra vertical component has zero net flux." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484470 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} 1-\\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=2\\pi$. The radial part contributes outward flow; the extra vertical component has zero net flux." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484492 1071 "**(a)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds\n$$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds.\n$$\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484492 1071 "**(a)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$.\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484492 1074 "Circulation: **positive**. Flux: **approximately zero**.\n\nThe arrows are upward along a path traversed from bottom to top in the right half-plane, so their tangential contribution is positive. The flux contributions on the lower and upper branches cancel: for the depicted vertical field with magnitude proportional to x, the right-normal flux is $$\n\\displaystyle -\\int x\\,dx,\n$$ whose endpoints have equal x-coordinates." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484492 1074 "Circulation: **positive**. Flux: **approximately zero**.\n\nThe arrows are upward along a path traversed from bottom to top in the right half-plane, so their tangential contribution is positive. The flux contributions on the lower and upper branches cancel: for the depicted vertical field with magnitude proportional to x, the right-normal flux is $-\\int x\\,dx$, whose endpoints have equal x-coordinates." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484497 1071 "**(c)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds\n$$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds.\n$$\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484497 1071 "**(c)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$.\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484505 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} 2\\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=0.\n$$ Inward and outward sectors cancel; the divergence is zero." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484505 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} 2\\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=0$. Inward and outward sectors cancel; the divergence is zero." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484510 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} -\\cos^2t\\,dt=-\\pi.\n$$ The radial part has no net circulation; the $-x$ vertical component produces a clockwise contribution." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484510 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} -\\cos^2t\\,dt=-\\pi$. The radial part has no net circulation; the $-x$ vertical component produces a clockwise contribution." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484532 1085 "Source question: Problem 1(a–c), PDF page 2.\n\nLet $C$ be the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$ and $f(x,y)=x^2+2y$. Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C f\\,ds,\n$$ using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C} f\\,ds\n$$ in part (c). The source states that scalar line integrals are independent of parametrization and orientation." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484532 1085 "Source question: Problem 1(a–c), PDF page 2.\n\nLet $C$ be the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$ and $f(x,y)=x^2+2y$. Calculate $\\int_C f\\,ds$, using $\\int_{-C} f\\,ds$ in part (c). The source states that scalar line integrals are independent of parametrization and orientation." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484556 1074 "Subtract row 1 from rows 2 and 3. This gives $y+4z=0$ and $-2y+2z=-1$, hence $10z=-1$, $z=-1/10$, $y=2/5$, and $x=6/5$. The RREF is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{-1}{10}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Substitution yields right-hand sides 2, 2, 1." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484556 1074 "Subtract row 1 from rows 2 and 3. This gives $y+4z=0$ and $-2y+2z=-1$, hence $10z=-1$, $z=-1/10$, $y=2/5$, and $x=6/5$. The RREF is $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{-1}{10}\\end{bmatrix}$. Substitution yields right-hand sides 2, 2, 1." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484566 1071 "**(a)** Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r\n$$ using $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Show the calculation.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484566 1071 "**(a)** Calculate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ using $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Show the calculation.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484566 1074 "$\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=\\langle t^2+2t,t+t^3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r\\prime=\\langle1,1\\rangle$. Thus $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1(t^2+3t+t^3)dt=1/3+3/2+1/4=25/12.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484566 1074 "$\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=\\langle t^2+2t,t+t^3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r\\prime=\\langle1,1\\rangle$. Thus $\\int_0^1(t^2+3t+t^3)dt=1/3+3/2+1/4=25/12$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484567 1074 "On $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, the outward normal is $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$. The flux is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi}[a\\cos^2t+(b+c)\\sin t\\cos t+d\\sin^2t]dt=\\pi(a+d).\n$$ Thus it is zero exactly when $a+d=0$, with b and c arbitrary. Independently, the divergence is $a+d$, so the flux form of Green’s theorem gives $(a+d)$ times the disk area $\\pi$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484567 1074 "On $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, the outward normal is $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$. The flux is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}[a\\cos^2t+(b+c)\\sin t\\cos t+d\\sin^2t]dt=\\pi(a+d)$. Thus it is zero exactly when $a+d=0$, with b and c arbitrary. Independently, the divergence is $a+d$, so the flux form of Green’s theorem gives $(a+d)$ times the disk area $\\pi$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484580 1071 "**(b)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds\n$$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds.\n$$\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484580 1071 "**(b)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$.\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484593 1074 "The speed is $\\sqrt2$ and $f(\\mathbf r(t))=t^2+2t$. Thus $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1(t^2+2t)\\sqrt2\\,dt=4\\sqrt2/3.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484593 1074 "The speed is $\\sqrt2$ and $f(\\mathbf r(t))=t^2+2t$. Thus $\\int_0^1(t^2+2t)\\sqrt2\\,dt=4\\sqrt2/3$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484596 1071 "**(b)** Prove $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=\\int_C\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.\n$$ State the normal convention.\n\nWritten response: {{response}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484596 1071 "**(b)** Prove $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=\\int_C\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$. State the normal convention.\n\nWritten response: {{response}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484600 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\cos^2t\\,dt=\\pi.\n$$ Upward vectors on the right and downward vectors on the left align with CCW travel, giving positive circulation." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484600 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} \\cos^2t\\,dt=\\pi$. Upward vectors on the right and downward vectors on the left align with CCW travel, giving positive circulation." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484611 1071 "**(a)** For the CCW circle of radius $a$ centered at the origin, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_Cx\\,dy\n$$ and compare it with the enclosed area.\n\n![Oriented boundary and dimensions](images/66/66c59485ae32ff038c4539f1e19950964319741bf7e642aca2a552c2a93a87c0.png)\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484611 1071 "**(a)** For the CCW circle of radius $a$ centered at the origin, calculate $\\int_Cx\\,dy$ and compare it with the enclosed area.\n\n![Oriented boundary and dimensions](images/66/66c59485ae32ff038c4539f1e19950964319741bf7e642aca2a552c2a93a87c0.png)\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484611 1074 "Use $x=a\\cos t,y=a\\sin t$. Then $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint x\\,dy=a^2\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos^2t\\,dt=\\pi a^2,\n$$ equal to the disk area. The arrow directions give positive (CCW) orientation." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484611 1074 "Use $x=a\\cos t,y=a\\sin t$. Then $\\oint x\\,dy=a^2\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos^2t\\,dt=\\pi a^2$, equal to the disk area. The arrow directions give positive (CCW) orientation." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484615 1071 "**(b)** For the plotted CCW square with opposite corners $(-1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_Cx\\,dy\n$$ and compare it with the enclosed area.\n\n![Oriented boundary and dimensions](images/fb/fbc268eb4de864e70cdf9bfcf0ce1b138631312f86ae3814516920c85df5e1c9.png)\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484615 1071 "**(b)** For the plotted CCW square with opposite corners $(-1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$, calculate $\\int_Cx\\,dy$ and compare it with the enclosed area.\n\n![Oriented boundary and dimensions](images/fb/fbc268eb4de864e70cdf9bfcf0ce1b138631312f86ae3814516920c85df5e1c9.png)\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484615 1074 "The horizontal edges have $dy=0$. The right edge contributes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,dy=2\n$$ and the left edge contributes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^{-1}(-1)\\,dy=2.\n$$ Total 4, equal to the square’s area. Green’s theorem also gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint x\\,dy=\\iint_D1\\,dA.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484615 1074 "The horizontal edges have $dy=0$. The right edge contributes $\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,dy=2$ and the left edge contributes $\\int_1^{-1}(-1)\\,dy=2$. Total 4, equal to the square’s area. Green’s theorem also gives $\\oint x\\,dy=\\iint_D1\\,dA$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484624 1071 "Source question: Exercise **1.2.48**, [printed page 47 (PDF page 59)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=59).\n\nFind the rank of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Show row reduction by hand.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484624 1071 "Source question: Exercise **1.2.48**, [printed page 47 (PDF page 59)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=59).\n\nFind the rank of $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$. Show row reduction by hand.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484624 1074 "The RREF is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ with pivots in columns 1, 3, 4. Rank is the number of pivots, hence 3. The fourth original row is the negative of the third, so rank cannot be 4." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484624 1074 "The RREF is $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$, with pivots in columns 1, 3, 4. Rank is the number of pivots, hence 3. The fourth original row is the negative of the third, so rank cannot be 4." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484625 1071 "**(b)** Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r\n$$ using $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^2,t^2\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Show the calculation.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484625 1071 "**(b)** Calculate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ using $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^2,t^2\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Show the calculation.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484773 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-9-9z}}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484773 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-9-9z}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484781 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{5x-50}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484781 1071 "$\\int \\sqrt{5x-50}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484799 1074 "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484799 1074 "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186484800 1074 "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186484800 1074 "First,